WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.700
So this is a new recent maths result! We've got&nbsp;
this here, a graph which is a network of points&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:09.660
and lines. So you could think of it like a&nbsp;
network of friends; a network of computers;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:14.760
London Underground map - these are all the classic&nbsp;
examples of networks, points and lines connecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:19.920
together. It's slightly unusual this graph; look, all&nbsp;
the points that you normally get in a graph, I've put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:23.940
them all in one line. And then you can see all&nbsp;
the connections being made between those points.&nbsp;

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:28.680
And when you do that, you know, these connections&nbsp;
these lines they cross each other, crisscrossing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:33.420
is everywhere. I wonder if we can uh uncross these?&nbsp;
Uncross them without keeping those points in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:39.060
straight line. I don't know. This is a way you could&nbsp;
uncross them; maybe if I took some of these lines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:44.600
maybe if I lift them up like that they don't&nbsp;
cross anymore.
- (Brady: Oh that's cheating isn't it?)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:52.680
And then maybe if I take this page here and lift&nbsp;
this up so they don't cross anymore - and what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:56.760
we've created is something called a book. 
- (Book?)&nbsp;
- What? Do you want me to do it in a Southern accent?

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:00.683
- (No it's alright)
- Book? I don't know how to do it in a Southern accent.

00:01:00.683 --> 00:01:06.672
It's a book - I'll do it proper Northern. It's a book&nbsp;
graph. So what we've done it's created a graph&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.672 --> 00:01:14.220
using four pages so that the lines now are&nbsp;
uncrossed; and this is special because this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:20.280
is the first one of its kind that shows&nbsp;
that this type of graph needs four pages

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:30.060
and it can't be done with fewer pages. I'll put&nbsp;
my book away and tell you a little bit more about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:36.060
book graphs. So that's the idea, so you're going&nbsp;
to put the points on a line; we're going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:41.700
try and draw our graph but we're going to try&nbsp;
and connect the points uh like their pages in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:46.440
book. Let's start with a simple example, I'll just&nbsp;
start with- I'll just do a square, four points. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:50.520
now let's turn that into a book and that means&nbsp;
I'm going to put the points along the spine of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:56.640
the book. And it's helpful to number these, let's do&nbsp;
1, 2, 3, 4 yeah. Now let's do 1, 2

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:02.580
3 and 4 all in a line where 1 connects to&nbsp;
2; 2 connects to 3; 3 connects to 4;

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:07.620
4 connects to 1 so it goes back like that.
That would be a one page book because I've just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:14.760
got this top half here that I'm using. So it's a&nbsp;
one-page book, it does depend on the order of your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:21.780
vertices here. So if I do a different order you&nbsp;
get a different book. Let's do 1, 3, 4, 2

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:27.240
then what do I need? 1 connects to 2 like&nbsp;
that; 2 connects to 3; 3 connects to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:32.580
4; 4 connects to 1. so I've created a two&nbsp;
page book. Let's see if we can find something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:38.640
needs three pages - we could do something like a&nbsp;
complete graph. A complete graph is when you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:42.960
the points and the lines and everything's&nbsp;
connected to everything else. So here is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:49.800
classic with five points, and I connect everything&nbsp;
to everything else. So it looks like this. Now let's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:54.360
say I'm going to try and minimise these crossings;&nbsp;
because that's kind of what I'm trying to do with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:58.560
this book as well. If you imagine these aren't like&nbsp;
elastic or string I could pull two of these lines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:03.840
out so I could do something that looks like this&nbsp;
and it will only have one crossing. Now if I want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:09.780
to draw that as a book this is how you would do it.&nbsp;
This pentagon is a loop so if I just name them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:16.980
that's 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5. I'm going to&nbsp;
draw out my points: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5. Now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:21.900
I'm going to do the loop first: so 1 connects to&nbsp;
2, connects to 3, connects to 4, connects to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:28.620
5, 5 connects to 1. And then anything inside&nbsp;
the loop I'll put on page 1. 2 connects to 4,

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:33.540
2 connects to 5 - and you can see I'm going to&nbsp;
have that crossing there. Maybe I'll save that as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:37.920
the last thing I do. So I've got 2 connects to&nbsp;
4, 2 connects to 5 like that and anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:42.720
like outside the loop I'll do on page 2. So what have
I got? 1 connects to 3 and 1 connects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:48.180
to 4. And then yes there was one line that I&nbsp;
missed which was 3 connects to 5. Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:54.300
I know that's going to cross and so I've got two&nbsp;
pages here looking lovely but yeah I'm gonna have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:59.220
to use three pages. I'll do it in a different colour,
3 connects to 5. So that would be my third&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:06.000
page. So this graph here needs three pages. If these&nbsp;
didn't cross I would have been able to do that in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:11.940
two pages. In fact you can make a book of any size;&nbsp;
if you've got a complete graph with n points&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:18.540
the book has n divided by 2 pages - or it's that&nbsp;
number rounded up. So if I wanted to make a book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:24.660
with 100 pages you just do the complete graph&nbsp;
with 200 points. So you can make a book of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:28.920
any size you want, there's nothing interesting&nbsp;
about that right so who cares. Like what might be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:34.260
more interesting though is um what about graphs&nbsp;
that can be untangled, right? Let's just look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:39.360
a specific kind of graph that's called a planar&nbsp;
graph when you can untangle it. Here we go. So if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:43.740
you've got a planar graph, if you can draw a&nbsp;
loop you can do it in two pages. I can do my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:48.420
five points again but maybe with less lines&nbsp;
so they don't cross over this time? So if I've got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:54.000
a planar graph which means they don't cross, and&nbsp;
I've got a loop, then I can make it two pages. Same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:00.660
as before: there's my loop, anything inside is page&nbsp;
1; anything outside is page 2. What about if we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:05.820
don't have a loop? Right, now there's a couple of&nbsp;
ways you can get around this. If you don't have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:11.280
loop you could do- I'm going to call it a phantom&nbsp;
loop. So it could be like this: there's no loops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:17.520
here, I'll call it 1, 2, 3 and 4. But I&nbsp;
can make a phantom loop if I want to: so I can go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:24.600
1 connects to 2, 2 connects to 3 and I'm&nbsp;
gonna put an imaginary 3 connects to 4 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:30.180
4 connects to 1. Then using that I can do&nbsp;
the same idea. Here's the book, and I can go 1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:37.140
connects to 2, 2 connects to 3; phantom&nbsp;
3 connects to 4, a phantom 4 connects to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:42.720
1 if I wanted to. And then what's inside that&nbsp;
loop? 2 connects the 4, so I will draw that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:47.400
on page 1. I would actually get a one page book&nbsp;
here. So you can turn things that don't have loops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:52.800
into something that has loops. So really now we're&nbsp;
interested in graphs that don't have crossings,

00:05:53.580 --> 00:06:00.780
that don't have loops, and you can't even do&nbsp;
phantom loops. So there are graphs like that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:04.213
as well. I'll show you an example of what something&nbsp;
like that would look like:

00:06:04.213 --> 00:06:05.580
(There it is)
- So we've got&nbsp;

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:12.060
a graph here: it's called the Goldner-Harary graph&nbsp;
and you can see it's got no crossings, and it has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:17.280
as many edges as you can put in it as well - at&nbsp;
least without creating any extra new crossings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:23.340
So we can't even put any phantom lines in this.&nbsp;
And this, if we turned that into our book, is going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:29.880
to be a three page book. Because we can't quite&nbsp;
use the trick I used before. So the three-page&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:34.500
book would look like this: so there we go we've got&nbsp;
our points in a line. We've tried our best, you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:39.180
we've connected it as a two-page book and then&nbsp;
we will have one extra line there that they've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:44.880
put in and that will have to go on the third&nbsp;
page. So this is an example of a planar graph&nbsp;

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:50.520
- which is a graph that has no crossings in it - that&nbsp;
needs three pages. Are there any graphs that need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:58.380
four pages? And that was the question. We knew that&nbsp;
there were no planar graphs that needed five pages;&nbsp;

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:06.420
so we knew the answer was going to be 3 as&nbsp;
a maximum or 4 as a maximum. And that was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:14.700
conjecture. And that was a conjecture for 30&nbsp;
years until uh 2020. So only recently uh we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:18.900
got our graph; and that's the graph I showed you at&nbsp;
the beginning so let's just take another look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:29.220
at it. It's got 275 points, it's got 819 edges. You&nbsp;
connect it up, it needs four pages and every order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.220 --> 00:07:36.360
as well need four pages - it can't be done better.&nbsp;
So this was- for the first time a planar graph&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:41.820
that needed four pages as a book; if you want to&nbsp;
see the original planar graph, so that it's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:45.858
points without any crossings in it. You'll&nbsp;
like this Brady.
- (Yeah I'm dying to see that)

00:07:45.858 --> 00:07:54.180
You'd love this. It's this beautiful heart-shaped&nbsp;
graph. It's got- I'll give you options: it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.180 --> 00:08:01.620
got 275 vertices, 819 edges, and it was found&nbsp;
by uh you know a combination of computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:08.040
search - obviously mathematical methods as well - and&nbsp;
they also used just purely mathematical methods&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:17.123
um to find a whole family of graphs like this
that can only be done in four pages and not&nbsp;better.
